+        "## Named mechanism: **Action Panel**\n- **Source claim:** Always-visible (or sticky) free-form command surface more discoverable than hidden menus; place near target; dynamic by selection/state; closable panels need easy reopen if sole home of actions (ch8 *Action Panel*; Fig 8-6\u20138-8).\n- **Observable trigger:** Many item/list actions only in right-click/menu with low discoverability; or panel far from operated objects.\n- **Required action:** Surface high-frequency/relevant verbs in panel adjacent to targets; keep panel contents state-synced to selection.\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** Users never find commands; wrong association of verb to noun.\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Small-screen density limits (source: not usually good for small devices); expert menu-bar apps with strong keyboard discoverability.\n- **Verification recipe:** static screenshot \u00b7 task test (first-use find-action)\n\n## Named mechanism: **Prominent \u201cDone\u201d Button** / **Assumed Next Step**\n- **Source claim:** Final commit of transaction/settings is a button (not link), strong treatment, label = user verb (Send/Buy/Install), placed at end of eye/task travel near last field (ch8 pattern; Fig 8-9\u20138-13). Secondary actions de-emphasized (ch10 forms cross-ref).\n- **Observable trigger:** Submit is a link or low-contrast control; or Done far from last input; multiple equal-weight CTAs at commit step.\n- **Required action:** One primary commit control at sequence end; verb label; secondary actions visually subordinate.\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** Abandoned purchase/form; \u201cwhat next?\u201d search; accidental secondary action.\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Multi-primary marketing pages (still differentiate hierarchy); wizard intermediate Next vs final Purchase.\n- **Verification recipe:** static screenshot \u00b7 task test (completion path)\n\n## Named mechanism: **Smart Menu Items**\n- **Source claim:** Menu/button labels dynamically name the object/operation (\u201cUndo Increase Clarity\u201d, \u201cAdd [person] to Contacts\u201d); disable when no object (ch8 *Smart Menu Items*; Fig 8-14 Lightroom, 8-15 Gmail).\n- **Observable trigger:** Generic \u201cDelete\u201d/\u201cUndo\u201d/\u201cAdd to contacts\u201d with ambiguous multi-object context; enabled with empty selection.\n- **Required action:** Interpolate concrete target into label; disable when no valid target; plurals for multi-select.\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** Wrong-object destructive action; users stop exploring.\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Extremely short mobile toolbars where space forces icons + tooltip only\u2014still need confirm/preview for high cost.\n- **Verification recipe:** state-transition test (selection changes label)\n\n## Named mechanism: **Preview**\n- **Source claim:** Before heavyweight/costly/hard-to-predict action, show sample outcome + commit + back-out/change from preview (ch8 *Preview*; Fig 8-16\u20138-18). Opposite of \u201cactivate then wait to see.\u201d\n- **Observable trigger:** Purchase, print, large open, irreversible filter/transform, multi-step form submit with no summary/WYSIWYG sample.\n- **Required action:** Show outcome summary/visual before commit; allow commit and back-out from same surface.\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** Costly errors; support load; distrust of filters/transforms.\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Instant, free, fully reversible micro-edits with multilevel undo; pure navigation.\n- **Verification recipe:** state-transition test \u00b7 task test\n\n## Named mechanism: **Spinners and Loading Indicators** (+ Norman/Nielsen latency bands)\n- **Source claim:** After submit, if response delayed: spinner (stateless) or loading indicator (proportion, remaining time, cancel). Bands: <0.1s live; 0.1\u20131s notice but wait; >1s risk \u201cbroken\u201d\u2014indicator mandatory (ch8 *Spinners\u2026*; Fig 8-19\u20138-23). Prefer keep UI alive; place cancel near indicator.\n- **Observable trigger:** Operation >~1s with frozen UI and no progress/spinner; or long job with spinner only when meter data available.\n- **Required action:** Show status + progress when known; spinner when unknown; do not lock UI if avoidable; surface cancel collocated.\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** Double-submit, abandon, \u201chung\u201d reports.\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Sub-100ms interactions (no chrome); background jobs with separate notification channel if primary UI stays responsive and status is findable.\n- **Verification recipe:** runtime metric (latency) \u00b7 state-transition test\n\n## Named mechanism: **Cancelability**\n- **Source claim:** Instant cancel of long op with no side effects; Stop/Cancel + stop icon; confirm cancel worked \u2264~1\u20132s; label which op when parallel (ch8 *Cancelability*; Fig 8-24\u20138-25). Supports **Safe Exploration**.\n- **Observable trigger:** Modal/long job >~2s without cancel; cancel leaves partial writes; cancel >2s to acknowledge.\n- **Required action:** Provide cancel; roll back or never commit partial; acknowledge immediately; disambiguate multi-job cancel.\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** Forced wait; stuck partial state; users fear exploration.\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Truly atomic remote commits already completed (show undo/reverse path instead); non-interruptible hardware ops\u2014still show honest \u201ccannot cancel\u201d state.\n- **Verification recipe:** state-transition test \u00b7 runtime metric\n\n## Named mechanism: **Multilevel Undo** / **reversible operations** / **action history stack**\n- **Source claim:** Model actions as discrete, named, reversible ops; stack LIFO \u226510\u201312; undo permanent-ish changes (edit, delete, layout, paste); do not put navigation/selection/scroll/window chrome on stack; impossible undos (sent mail, forum post, purchase) must be obvious (ch8 *Multilevel Undo*; Fig 8-26). Hard to retrofit\u2014architecture first.\n- **Observable trigger:** Highly interactive editor/mail/graphics app with only single-level undo or none; irreversible file/object deletes without undo; undo labeled generically without next op (tie to Smart Menu Items).\n- **Required action:** Implement reversible command model + multilevel stack + smart undo labels; exclude pure view/nav from stack.\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** Fear of exploration; catastrophic recovery (reload/backup); creative flow broken.\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Simple form/nav apps; external side effects that cannot reverse\u2014use Preview/confirm + compensating action instead of false Undo.\n- **Verification recipe:** state-transition test \u00b7 task test (N undos)\n"
