+        "## Named mechanism: **MECE** (Mutually Exclusive, Collectively Exhaustive)\n- **Source claim:** Categories must be clearly distinct (no confusing overlap) and together complete for intended content + use cases; expandable without confusion; categories become nav foundation (ch2 \u201cMutually Exclusive, Collectively Exhaustive\u201d; site maps/content outlines as docs).\n- **Observable trigger:** Nav/sitemap labels where same item fits two top-level buckets, or a supported task/object has no home; ambiguous mega-menu cells.\n- **Required action:** Rewrite taxonomy so each item has one primary home; every in-scope content/task maps to a place; document with site map/outline.\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** Users can\u2019t decide where to look/put things; nav feels arbitrary; growth breaks structure (ch2 MECE).\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Secondary associative paths (tags, related content, search hits) may multi-link; **MECE** binds the *primary* organization scheme, not every cross-link.\n- **Verification recipe:** static sitemap audit \u2014 sample N objects/tasks; each has exactly one primary path + \u22651 path for every in-scope case.\n\n## Named mechanism: **Frequency of use** (visibility by demand)\n- **Source claim:** Frequent tasks/controls/topics must be immediately available without search/browse; rare items may be buried (settings, help) (ch2 \u201cMake Frequently Used Items Visible\u201d). Ch3: elevate frequent actions into top-level **global navigation** independent of formal hierarchy depth; bury infrequent behind submenu/accordion/tab (ch3 \u201cPut frequently accessed items directly in the global navigation\u201d).\n- **Observable trigger:** High-use action (analytics/task list) requires multi-level drill while rare settings share equal prominence; global nav reflects org chart not use frequency.\n- **Required action:** Rank by expected frequency; promote top-N to always-visible global/primary chrome; demote low-N behind progressive disclosure.\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** Everyday work costs repeated search/browse; experts still pay novice path cost.\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Safety/legal items may need high visibility despite low frequency; expert **accelerators** (shortcuts) complement but don\u2019t replace visible frequent paths for mixed audiences (ch2 novice/experienced).\n- **Verification recipe:** task test / runtime metric \u2014 top tasks \u2264 target clicks/taps vs baseline; rare settings \u22651 extra disclosure step.\n",
+        "## Named mechanism: **Context-switch cost** + **keep distances short**\n- **Source claim:** Opening page/window incurs cognitive cost (reorient shape/layout/exits); even familiar pages cost; load latency reduces exploration \u2014 \u201clatency costs viewers\u201d (ch3 Cognitive Load). Minimize taps/clicks A\u2192B: broad top-level (flatten hierarchy), put frequent items in global nav, bring common tasks onto one screen \u2014 aim most common ~80% use cases one page without context switches; avoid multi-page simple tasks especially with step dependencies (ch3 Keep Distances Short + subsections). Multilevel tree needs \u22652 jumps subpage\u2192subpage; convert toward fully-connected via **Fat Menus** / **Sitemap Footer** (ch3 Multilevel or Tree; Fat Menus/Sitemap Footer Why).\n- **Observable trigger:** Common task needs many routes/dialogs; deep tree without full cross-links; top nav only 2\u20133 items forcing long cascade; measured multi-hop for high-frequency paths.\n- **Required action:** Flatten/broaden primary nav; co-locate dependent steps; convert multilevel to single-jump reachability for major pages (fat menu or sitemap footer) when hierarchy is deep.\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** Accumulated reorientation + wasted backtracks on missing preconditions; abandoned slow pages (ch3).\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Focused modes (fullscreen slideshow) should *strip* global nav \u2014 short distance \u2260 always show everything; use Back/Next + **Escape Hatch** (ch3 Flat Navigation note on modes). Hub-and-spoke mobile when spokes can\u2019t fit full global nav (ch3 Hub and Spoke).\n- **Verification recipe:** runtime metric / task test \u2014 hop count for top tasks; max depth of primary IA; major pages reachable in \u22641 jump from any major page if claiming fully-connected.\n\n## Named mechanism: **Clear Entry Points**\n- **Source claim:** Few main entry \u201cdoors,\u201d task- or audience-oriented, clear CTAs; first-time/infrequent users; smaller specialized links must not out-compete front doors (ch3 Clear Entry Points What/How; Fig 3-7). Skip if purpose already clear and extra step would irritate intermediates/experts.\n- **Observable trigger:** First screen is dense undifferentiated chrome/ads/disabled toolbars with no primary start tasks; or many equal-weight doors.\n- **Required action:** 1\u2013few dominant, plain-language entry actions covering most arrival goals; demote secondary nav visually.\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** \u201cOK, here I am. Now what?\u201d \u2014 hesitant users don\u2019t begin (ch3 Why).\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Expert-first tools where extra entry step is pure friction (ch3 Use when).\n- **Verification recipe:** static screenshot + first-run task test \u2014 new users pick correct start without hunting.\n\n## Named mechanism: **Escape Hatch**\n- **Source claim:** Well-labeled control from limited-nav screens (wizard, **Modal Panel**, deep-link landing, error/dead-end) back to safe known place (home/hub/full-nav) (ch3 Escape Hatch; wayfinding \u201ctrump card\u201d). Supports safe exploration; prevents app abandon; logo-home, Cancel as hatch (How/Examples).\n- **Observable trigger:** Modal/wizard/error/SERP-landed page with no path to full-nav hub; trapped serial flow without cancel/home.\n- **Required action:** Always-visible labeled hatch to safe place on limited-nav and error screens; deep-link targets must include hatch (Why).\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** Felt trap \u2192 hard exit (close app); can\u2019t recover from wrong branch/deep link (ch3).\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Fully-connected pages already showing global nav (hatch redundant as separate control; home logo still fine).\n- **Verification recipe:** state-transition test \u2014 from modal/wizard/404/deep-link, one control returns to hub with full nav.\n\n## Named mechanism: **Breadcrumbs** (hierarchy context) vs **Progress Indicator** (linear map)\n- **Source claim:** **Breadcrumbs** = parent\u2013child slice of IA hierarchy \u2014 \u201cyou are here\u201d relative to site, *not* click-history; essential after search/faceted/deep-link landings when global nav can\u2019t show deep position (ch3 Breadcrumbs What/Why; Target Fig 3-55). **Progress Indicator** = map of linear sequence with you-are-here (+ optional jump back); for Wizard/narrative/process; shows remaining work; hierarchical topology \u2192 breadcrumbs instead (ch3 Progress Indicator Use when/Why/How).\n- **Observable trigger:** Hierarchy depth \u22652 without breadcrumb trail; linear checkout/wizard without step map; breadcrumbs used as Back-stack history only; progress map without titles when steps differ in kind.\n- **Required action:** Deep hierarchical/content IA \u2192 **Breadcrumbs** (titles as links up). Linear prescribed process \u2192 **Progress Indicator** with current/done states and short step titles (numbers alone weak).\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** Deep-landed users lack context (breadcrumbs) or can\u2019t estimate finish / re-enter prior step (progress) (ch3).\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Flat 1-level sites; pure tool **flat navigation**; tiny 2-step flows may skip full map if remaining work is obvious (still show step identity).\n- **Verification recipe:** screenshot + deep-link task \u2014 land mid-tree via search; breadcrumb shows path; wizard shows remaining steps accurately.\n\n## Named mechanism: **Deep Links** (location + state in URL)\n- **Source claim:** Persist location *and* relevant app state in URL/bookmarkable link (position, layers, zoom, etc.); reload restores; shareable; mobile app-to-app without losing task context (ch3 Deep Links). Don\u2019t capture settings that would stomp user prefs \u2014 scenario-design what belongs in link (How).\n- **Observable trigger:** Interactive content (map, document page, filtered results, multi-step mid-flow) with non-shareable/ephemeral state; refresh loses place; no permalink control.\n- **Required action:** Encode restorable position+chosen view state in URL; document which state is linkable vs session-local.\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** Forced multi-hop re-find; can\u2019t share exact view; mobile context loss (ch3 Why).\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Private/security-sensitive state must not go in URL; pure ephemeral drafts may stay session-only.\n- **Verification recipe:** state-transition test \u2014 copy URL, new session/device restores intended view without re-traversing IA.\n\n## Named mechanism: **Modal Panel** (forced focus) + **Animated Transition** (orientation under change)"
