+        "## Named mechanism: **Goal-filtered perception** (scan, not read)\n- **Source claim:** Goals **guide** sampling and **filter** preconscious notice; unrelated items often never register (scissors task; Canterbury \u201cwin $100\u201d missed). Adults more goal-filtered than children. Ch1 \u201cPerception Biased by Goals,\u201d Figs 1.8\u20131.9, pp. 8\u201312; takeaways.\n- **Observable trigger:** Plan/copy assumes users \u201cwill see\u201d banner, promo, side-nav, or secondary legal text while pursuing unrelated primary goal; success path relies on non-goal-aligned chrome.\n- **Required action:** Map each step\u2019s user goal; place needed info/actions **goal-scented** (labels match goal words), prominent on path; never sole-path on goal-orthogonal chrome.\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** High miss rate on \u201cobvious\u201d elements; support tickets \u201cfeature missing.\u201d\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Exploration/browsing tasks without sharp goal; training modes that force full scan.\n- **Verification recipe:** Task test with assigned goal + free report of non-goal elements. Eye-tracking optional.\n\n## Named mechanism: **Gestalt proximity**\n- **Source claim:** Relative distance drives perceived subgroups; near objects group even if dissimilar. Prefer spacing over group-boxes; label\u2013control gap must bind correct pair (Delta vs United radios). Ch2 Proximity, Figs 2.1\u20132.5, pp. 16\u201318.\n- **Observable trigger:** Label closer to neighbor control than own; form sections use boxes for single control; related controls spaced like unrelated.\n- **Required action:** Encode intended groups by spacing hierarchy first; label closer to target than to distractors; remove redundant single-item group boxes.\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** Wrong radio selected; users can\u2019t find which label maps which field.\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Dense data tables where grid lines are the structure (still verify cell-label binding); a11y still needs programmatic association.\n- **Verification recipe:** Static screenshot \u2014 measure label\u2013control vs label\u2013neighbor distances. DOM: `label[for]` matches visual proximity.\n\n## Named mechanism: **Gestalt similarity**\n- **Source claim:** Similar appearance \u2192 perceived group (bold unread mail; car-shape rides). Unintended similarity creates false groups (two similar menus). Ch2 Similarity, Figs 2.6\u20132.8, pp. 18\u201320.\n- **Observable trigger:** Same style on unrelated actions; critical status not uniquely styled; parallel widgets look identical but mean different things.\n- **Required action:** Shared look only for shared semantics; status/unread/availability via consistent distinctive feature; break false groups by differentiation.\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** Users treat unrelated controls as one set; miss status that doesn\u2019t pop.\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Brand-uniform chrome where all chrome is non-semantic; intentional monochrome systems using position alone (then proximity must carry load).\n- **Verification recipe:** Static screenshot Gestalt audit: list perceived groups vs intended.\n\n## Named mechanism: **Gestalt continuity / closure / symmetry / common fate / figure\u2013ground**\n- **Source claim:** Alignment fills gaps into continuous forms (sliders, IBM logo); incomplete figures close (stacks); complex scenes simplify to symmetric wholes (tables); co-motion groups; smaller/overlapping = figure. Combined principles can imply **unintended** relations \u2014 audit all seven. Ch2 Continuity\u2013Common Fate + Combined, Figs 2.9\u20132.23, pp. 20\u201329.\n- **Observable trigger:** Aligned but unrelated elements; partial stack implying more items than exist; animated co-motion of unrelated entities; modal/overlay without clear figure elevation; table-less prose for comparable multi-attribute data.\n- **Required action:** Align only related; stack affordance only if collection real; co-animate only related; overlays as temporary figure over preserved ground; comparable data in symmetric table structure.\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** False continuity/group; users invent objects/relations not in data; lose place after replace-not-overlay.\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Decorative motion with no semantic claim; intentionally abstract art UI.\n- **Verification recipe:** Static screenshot Gestalt checklist (7 principles). Animation: common-fate audit. DOM for heading/table structure.\n"
