+      "sections": [
+        "## Named mechanism: **Preview first, select and initiate, then manage execution** (+ predictive planning displays)\n- **Source claim:** HCAI pattern 2 (Table 9.2; ch9): for temporal/robot processes\u2014**preview** entire process, user selects plan, initiates, then **manages execution**. Navigation tools, cameras. Mercedes parking assist step-by-step intended actions; better: show expected path; Brooks & Szafir: **predictive planning displays** \u2192 higher accuracy + satisfaction (ch8).\n- **Observable trigger:** Multi-step automated plan (drive path, robot trajectory, batch irreversible ops, agent tool chain) that starts without preview of intended steps/path and without mid-execution manage/cancel.\n- **Required action:** Before commit: show plan/path/preview; require select/initiate; during: live status + modify/cancel; prefer predictive spatial/step display over opaque \u201cworking\u2026\u201d.\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** User cannot accept/reject plan; surprise actuations; lower task accuracy and trust (ch8).\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Single reversible keystroke autocomplete fully ignorable (Ex 9.5\u20139.6); sub-second hard-real-time safety actuation (airbag)\u2014preview impossible; use pre-certified behavior + post-event log.\n- **Verification recipe:** Screenshot of preview; state-transition: preview \u2192 initiate \u2192 manage \u2192 complete/cancel; task test accuracy with vs without preview.\n\n## Named mechanism: **Interactive control panels** (steer; immediate feedback)\n- **Source claim:** News recommender: three sliders; list updates **immediately** as users move them \u2192 more clicks + strong desire for control (ch6). Pattern 3: **Steer by way of interactive control panels** (Table 9.2; ch9). Thermostat: see state, reset, hear/see system respond, see goal achieved (Ex 9.1; ch9). Golden Rules: informative feedback; keep users in control (Table 9.1).\n- **Observable trigger:** Preference/automation parameters exist only as one-shot config or black-box personalization with no live steering UI; or control change without visible system response.\n- **Required action:** For preference-shaped AI, provide interactive controls (sliders/toggles/panels) with **immediate** result update; always show current state + that intent was recognized.\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** Users stuck with opaque recommendations; lower engagement/acceptance; false \u201cautomation vs augmentation\u201d choice (ch9 Google guidebook critique).\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Controls that are pure noise for the task (ch6: poorly designed unused controls \u2260 argument against good ones); latency-bound batch jobs\u2014show queued state + ETA, not fake instant.\n- **Verification recipe:** DOM: controls bound to result region; task test: change control \u2192 result changes without page-rebuild dead-end; screenshot before/after slider.\n\n## Named mechanism: **Interlocks, guards, and safe defaults**\n- **Source claim:** Interlocks/guards prevent deadly mistakes (oven door, reverse thrusters only after touchdown, positive train control) (ch8). Excessive human control via complex panels + **unsafe defaults** is design failure, not \u201cuser error\u201d (Thimbleby IV overdose\u2014ch8). PCA: patient trigger *plus* lockout/total-dose interlocks; upper-right adds ML dosing + explanation + hospital monitoring (Figs 8.6\u20138.8; ch8). Formal methods / range checks generalize guards (ch8).\n- **Observable trigger:** Destructive or clinical automation with defaults that select high-risk options; missing range locks; human can command physically impossible/unsafe state; or \u201cuser control\u201d UI with no safety envelope.\n- **Required action:** Safe-by-default settings; hard interlocks on impossible/unsafe commands; allow user control *inside* envelope; document lockout reasons in UI.\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** Deadly \u201chuman error\u201d that is design failure; or excessive automation without envelope (MAX); or excessive unconstrained control (medication overdose).\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Mastery activities where error is part of learning (upper-left: bike, piano\u2014ch8)\u2014soft guards only; emergency break-glass with audit (hospital drive / firefighter elevator keys\u2014ch6, ch8).\n- **Verification recipe:** State-transition: unsafe command rejected; default path = safe; review of default config values against hazard analysis.\n\n## Named mechanism: **Comprehensible, predictable, controllable** interfaces (status \u2192 mental model \u2192 stop)\n- **Source claim:** Successful designs are **comprehensible, predictable, and controllable**, raising self-efficacy \u2192 reliable/safe/trustworthy (ch9). Users adopt tech that shows **current status** on a control panel, supports a **mental model to predict future actions**, allows **stop** of unintelligible actions (ch7). Eight Golden Rules still apply to HCAI (Table 9.1). Non-intrusive optional AI: red squiggle / translation choices leave user deciding if/when to accept (Ex 9.5\u20139.6; ch9). Visual UIs that prevent/reduce need for post-hoc explainability (ch7).\n- **Observable trigger:** AI feature changes state with no status indicator, no predictable next-step cue, no cancel; or auto-applies irreversible edits without optional accept; or \u201cexplainability\u201d only as post-hoc essay with no live status/control.\n- **Required action:** For each AI action: show state, make next action predictable, provide stop/undo; prefer optional suggestions over silent auto-commit for reversible content work.\n- **Predicted failure if ignored:** Deskilling + surprise automation; users cannot intervene; trustworthiness perceived as magic not control (ch8 vigilance/deskilling).\n- **Exemptions / counterexamples:** Auto-save of drafts with undo history is OK; silent auto-send/delete/deploy is not. Full computer autonomy region only when pre-justified rapid-action (Fig 8.3).\n- **Verification recipe:** Screenshot status + next-step; state-transition stop/undo; task test: user predicts next automation step before it runs.\n"
